Two right triangles share a side as follows: [asy]
pair pA, pB, pC, pD, pE;
pA = (0, 0);
pB = pA + 4 * dir(0);
pC = pA + 5 * dir(90);
pD = pB + 4 * dir(90);
pE = (4 * pA + 5 * pD) / 9;
draw(pA--pB--pC--pA);
draw(pA--pB--pD--pA);
label("$A$", pA, SW);
label("$B$", pB, SE);
label("$C$", pC, NW);
label("$D$", pD, NE);
label("$E$", pE, 2 * N);
label("$4$", pA--pB, S);
label("$5$", pA--pC, W);
label("$4$", pB--pD, E);
draw(rightanglemark(pB,pA,pC,8));
draw(rightanglemark(pD,pB,pA,8));
[/asy] What is the area of $\triangle ABE$?

Rectangle $ABCD$ has center $O$ and $AB/AD=k$. A point is randomly chosen from the interior of rectangle $ABCD$. What is the probability that it is closer to $O$ than to any of the four vertices?  [asy]
size(200);
draw((-250,100)--(250,100)--(250,-100)--(-250,-100)--cycle);
dot((0,0));
label("$O$",(0,0),N);
label("$A$",(-250,100),NW); label("$B$",(250,100),NE); label("$C$",(250,-100),SE); label("$D$",(-250,-100),SW);[/asy]

response:
Let us first draw the diagonals of the rectangle, which intersect at $O$. [asy]
size(200);
draw((-250,100)--(250,100)--(250,-100)--(-250,-100)--cycle);
draw((-250,100)--(250,-100)); draw((250,100)--(-250,-100));
dot((0,0));
label("$O$",(0,0),N);
label("$A$",(-250,100),NW); label("$B$",(250,100),NE); label("$C$",(250,-100),SE); label("$D$",(-250,-100),SW);[/asy]

We can see that the set of points closer to $O$ than any of the vertices is the same as the set of points inside the square with side length $AB/2$ and center $O$. [asy]
size(200);
draw((-250,100)--(250,100)--(250,-100)--(-250,-100)--cycle);
draw((-250,100)--(250,-100)); draw((250,100)--(-250,-100));
draw((-50,100)--(50,-100),dashed);
dot((0,0));
label("$O$",(0,0),N);
label("$A$",(-250,100),NW); label("$B$",(250,100),NE); label("$C$",(250,-100),SE); label("$D$",(-250,-100),SW);
draw((-125,100)--(125,-100),dashed);
[/asy]

Since $AB/AD=k$, the side length of the smaller square is $AB/2=k/2$. The area of the smaller square is $(k/2)^2=k^2/4$. The area of the larger square is $AB\cdot AD=k\cdot 1=k$. Therefore, the probability that the point is closer to $O$ than any of the vertices is $\frac{k^2/4}{k}=\boxed{\frac{k}{4}}$.
Final Answer: The final answer is $\frac{k}{4}$. I hope it is correct. 
